From: apklayer
Subject: libbinder: an app JVM's caller uid is the work source of its request header

All app JVMs run as the desktop user's one Linux uid, so a service that keys its data by the kernel's calling uid (keystore2: one key
namespace per uid) would give every app the same namespace. Each app JVM writes its Android uid as the work source of the request header
(runtime/host/java/apklayer/host/ShadowHostRealParcel.java); IPCThreadState::getCallingUid answers it for a caller with this process's own
Linux uid. Native callers write no work source (kUnsetWorkSource) and keep the kernel uid. Rebuild: `m libbinder`.
Against frameworks/native 46018f54b424 (REVISION), after 0001-0004. Apply: patches/apply aosp <LineageOS tree>.
---
--- a/libs/binder/IPCThreadState.cpp
+++ b/libs/binder/IPCThreadState.cpp
@@ -460,6 +460,12 @@
 uid_t IPCThreadState::getCallingUid() const
 {
     checkContextIsBinderForUse(__func__);
+    // apklayer patch: every app JVM runs as the desktop user's Linux uid and names its Android uid as the work source of its request headers
+    // (apklayer.host.ShadowHostRealParcel), which Parcel::enforceInterface stores; such a caller is known by that uid, so services that key
+    // their data by the calling uid (keystore2) keep apps apart. A cleared identity (no kernel caller) and a work source this thread
+    // propagates itself are not a caller's.
+    if (mCallingUid.has_value() && mCallingUid.value() == getuid() && mWorkSource != kUnsetWorkSource && !mPropagateWorkSource)
+        return static_cast<uid_t>(mWorkSource);
     return mCallingUid.has_value() ? mCallingUid.value() : getuid();
 }
 
